description:
## What is wrong
`evb-relay --robot --format toon` exits with code 5 and message:
  invalid robot format "toon": only json is allowed as an explicit override

The plan (Step 10 format matrix) says: "With --robot, only the default TOON output and --format json are valid." This reads as two valid options: TOON (implicit default) and json (explicit override). A user or agent explicitly requesting `--format toon` in robot mode should get the same result as omitting `--format` — not an error.

## Where
cli/internal/format/format.go:18-26 — the robot-mode branch only accepts empty string or "json".

## Fix
Add `normalized == TOON` as a valid case in the robot branch of `Resolve()`:
```go
if robot {
    if normalized == "" || normalized == TOON {
        return TOON, nil
    }
    if normalized == JSON {
        return JSON, nil
    }
    return "", fmt.Errorf(...)
}
```

## Impact
Low — only affects users who explicitly spell out `--format toon --robot` instead of relying on the default. But agents reading `--robot-capabilities` output see `robot_default_format: toon` and may reasonably pass it back explicitly.

## What is wrong
rest_api.c:237-238 — when a relay ID is invalid (unparseable, zero,
or > RELAY_COUNT), the function sends a 404 error response then
returns the result of rest_api_send_error():

  if (!parsed || (parsed_id == 0U) || (parsed_id > RELAY_COUNT)) {
      return rest_api_send_error(req, 404, "RELAY_NOT_FOUND", ...);
  }

rest_api_send_error returns ESP_OK when the HTTP send succeeds. So
the caller (e.g., rest_api_onboard_relay_set_handler) sees ESP_OK
and CONTINUES processing:

  err = rest_api_parse_onboard_relay_id(req, ..., &relay_id);
  if (err != ESP_OK) {  // ESP_OK → falls through!
      return err;
  }
  // relay_id is still 0 (never set)
  err = relay_set(0, requested_state);  // id=0 → ESP_ERR_INVALID_ARG
  return rest_api_send_error(req, 500, "RELAY_SET_FAILED", ...);
  // ↑ SECOND response on same connection!

## Impact
Every PUT/POST to an invalid onboard relay ID (e.g.,
/api/v1/relays/onboard/99) produces TWO HTTP responses: first a
correct 404, then a spurious 500. This violates the HTTP protocol
and corrupts the connection. The device test passes because it only
checks for the 404 substring in the response buffer.

Affects: rest_api_onboard_relay_set_handler,
rest_api_onboard_relay_toggle_handler

## Fix
rest_api_parse_onboard_relay_id should return an error code that
tells the caller to stop, not the httpd send result:

  if (!parsed || ...) {
      rest_api_send_error(req, 404, "RELAY_NOT_FOUND", ...);
      return ESP_FAIL;
  }

The caller's `if (err != ESP_OK) return err;` then stops correctly.
Alternatively, return a sentinel like ESP_ERR_NOT_FOUND.

## What is wrong
firmware/test/test_main.c:13-21 — setUp() resets gpio, i2c, nvs, and
component stubs, but does NOT call esp_event_stub_reset().

The esp_event stub in esp_event_stubs.c has static state:
- s_post_result (default return value for esp_event_post)
- s_last_base, s_last_id, s_last_data (last posted event)

The relay component calls esp_event_post on every relay_set/toggle.
If a test ever sets s_post_result to an error via
esp_event_stub_set_post_result(), that error persists into
subsequent tests, causing spurious failures.

Additionally, tests that inspect the last-posted event
(s_last_base/id/data) via esp_event_stub_get_last_*() would see
stale data from a prior test's relay operations.

## Fix
Add esp_event_stub_reset() to setUp() alongside the other stub
resets:
  void setUp(void) {
      gpio_stub_reset();
      i2c_stub_reset();
      nvs_stub_reset();
+     esp_event_stub_reset();
      device_config_reset_for_testing();
      mod_io_reset_for_testing();
      relay_reset_for_testing();
  }

## What is wrong
firmware/test_app/main/test_main.c:11-27 — tearDown() calls
TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL on relay_init, relay_set, mod_io_set_relays, etc.

When a test body fails and tearDown also encounters an error (e.g.,
relay_set returns an error because relay_init was never called in that
test), Unity reports BOTH failures. The tearDown failure adds noise
to the test output, making it harder to spot the actual failure.

Example output after a relay test fails:
  test_relay_device.c:50: FAIL: Expected 0 Was 1    ← real failure
  test_main.c:14: FAIL: Expected ESP_OK Was 0x103   ← tearDown noise

## Fix
Replace assertions in tearDown with conditional checks:
  void tearDown(void) {
      if (relay_init() == ESP_OK) {
          relay_set(1U, false);
          relay_set(2U, false);
      }
      if (mod_io_is_present()) {
          mod_io_set_relays(0x00U);
      }
  }

This ensures cleanup runs best-effort without adding assertion
failures to the test output.

The i2c_stubs.c transmit failure mechanism uses a one-shot flag (s_transmit_fail_once) that auto-clears after the first i2c_master_transmit() call. There is no way to make all transmits fail persistently, as would happen with a truly absent I2C device (every START+address would NACK).

This means the code path in mod_io_read_relay_mask_locked() where the i2c_master_transmit() of the command byte fails (line 174-178) is never exercised by any test. All absent-device tests simulate absence by letting the transmit succeed and failing only the receive via s_transmit_receive_result. In real hardware, an absent device NACKs at the address byte during transmit, so receive is never reached.

Untested path in mod_io.c:
  err = i2c_master_transmit(s_state.device_handle, &command, sizeof(command), ...);
  if (err != ESP_OK) {
      return err;  // <-- never reached in tests for absent-device scenario
  }

Current tests for absent device (e.g. test_mod_io_probe_keeps_absent_state_when_board_is_missing) set s_transmit_receive_result = ESP_ERR_NOT_FOUND, which fails the i2c_master_receive() call, but the preceding i2c_master_transmit() succeeds — unlike real hardware behavior.

Fix: Add a persistent transmit failure mode to i2c_stubs (e.g. i2c_stub_set_transmit_result_persistent(esp_err_t)) that fails all transmits until reset, and add a test case that verifies mod_io_probe_locked correctly handles transmit-stage failure for absent devices.

## What is wrong

In `firmware/components/auth/auth.c`, the `auth_constant_time_equals` function is intended to compare the API tokens in constant time to prevent timing attacks. However, the loop bound `max_len` is calculated as `max_len = (lhs_len > rhs_len) ? lhs_len : rhs_len;`. 

If an attacker provides an `rhs` (the presented token) of varying lengths, the execution time will increase linearly once the length of `rhs` exceeds the length of `lhs` (the secret configured token). Conversely, if the length of `rhs` is less than or equal to `lhs`, the execution time will be constant (bound by `lhs_len`). This allows an attacker to deduce the exact length of the configured API token by observing the inflection point in the execution time.

## Why this matters

While the contents of the token remain protected from a direct timing attack, leaking the length of a secret token reduces its entropy and compromises the strict constant-time guarantee of the authentication mechanism.

## Suggested fix

To be truly constant time, the loop must always execute a fixed, data-independent number of iterations. It should iterate exactly `DEVICE_CONFIG_API_TOKEN_MAX_LEN` times.

```c
    for (size_t i = 0; i < DEVICE_CONFIG_API_TOKEN_MAX_LEN; ++i) {
        unsigned char lhs_ch = (i < lhs_len) ? (unsigned char)lhs[i] : 0U;
        unsigned char rhs_ch = (i < rhs_len) ? (unsigned char)rhs[i] : 0U;

        diff |= (unsigned)(lhs_ch ^ rhs_ch);
    }
```
